  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v265: EVENT IDENTITY, PHASE I0 — the fold-semantics contract (C3) and the merge/split rules (C4), as pure functions with exhaustive fixtures. VERSION SLOT: the next free number after v264. v259 remains a deliberate hole and is not reused.\n\nNOTHING YOU CAN SEE CHANGES, ON PURPOSE. This release adds two modules that nothing calls yet, two fixture files and two test files. `temporal_timeline` is untouched, no claim gains a field, no record is written anywhere, and `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` stays `timeline-rules:4` — `timeline-rules:5` belongs to phase I1, where grouping actually changes and every fingerprint moves with it. A test asserts that, so a later branch cannot borrow the version early.\n\nWHY A PHASE THAT SHIPS NO BEHAVIOR. The event-identity program answers the owner's own sentence — “if I talk about the same thing in five different ways and I provide facts for them, it doesn't correlate them with the same thing” — and the plan it comes from (lifehug-platform `docs/design/event-identity.md` v4) was audited three times before a line was written. The auditor's standing condition was that authority, identity, migration and recovery semantics may not be decided for the first time inside the implementation. I0 is that condition met: the decisions written down as pure functions, each proved against fixtures, so I1 wires an already-proven contract into a 4,000-line fold instead of deciding grouping, ids, entailment and containment while editing it.\n\nC3, `system/episode_fold_contract.py` — WHAT GROUPS WITH WHAT. An active `same` binding decides a claim's grouping key; `GROUPING_RULE_TEXT` is the rule written down in the module rather than described, and its six reasons are closed and each reached by a fixture. The two era cases the audit demanded are both fixtures and sit beside each other, because the difference between them is the whole contract: a telling ABOUT an era — somebody naming or bounding College — is refused as a binding target, while a telling about an event WITHIN an era keeps full episode eligibility, since a membership is a receipt about a node and never a reason to discard a binding. A telling whose claims disagree about which of the two they are is a refusal, never a partial bind. WHICH ID IT PUBLISHES: the frozen minter, unchanged — `derive_node_id(node_kind=\"episode\", canonical_event_kind, subject_keys, discriminator=episode_id)` — with `episode_id` and `node_id` published as two identifiers and one durable mapping, never one identifier wearing two names, and every telling's former key landing in `node_aliases`. WHAT A NEGATIVE ENTAILS: `same(A,E) ∧ not_same(B,E) ⇒ not_same(A,B)`, computed at fold time and never stored expanded, with the retraction test that says why. WHAT A CONTAINMENT DRAWS: the same discipline `place_co_location` established in v264, which is why it is written against `chronology.DateRecord` and not a shape of its own — the bounds are the episode's own bounds copied, the rule reads `best is None` and nothing else, `anchors` is empty, the episode's provenance is dropped, and the member keeps its own precision question, better anchored rather than suppressed.\n\nC4, `system/episode_routing_contract.py` — WHERE EVERYTHING GOES WHEN AN EPISODE SPLITS OR MERGES. “A split restores the prior drawing” is only true before the person has labelled, dragged, answered or opened anything, so the contract is a table (`SPLIT_REFERENCE_RULES`) with one test per row: an ordering constraint re-attaches to the side carrying its anchor claim; era memberships, display roles and the label stay with the surviving episode id and the departing telling reverts to its own node with none; work items re-key through `work_item_aliases` and open sessions through `node_aliases`. The governing clause is tested over the whole table at once: NO POST-MERGE DECISION IS EVER COPIED TO BOTH SIDES, and anything unattributable — an unenumerated reference kind included — becomes one inspectable Mirror judgment row naming both candidates. A merge decides nothing and instead proves the envelope moved everything: a membership left live, or an absorbed id missing from `aliases_created`, is `merge_envelope_incomplete`. A delayed answer has three outcomes and the third is the point — a pair whose TELLING re-keyed away is acknowledged and dropped with a note, never re-pointed at whatever telling now occupies that source.\n\nTHE HONEST DETERMINISM CONTRACT. Two properties and only two: a fixed set of receipts folds byte-identically under any application order (the test permutes claims × bindings × manifest rows against one fingerprint), and incremental arrival converges without being order-independent. There is deliberately NO partition-equality test in this repository and no “never an over-merge” claim: v2 promised the first, v3 the second, and the audit showed the incremental binder delivers neither. What the contract guarantees instead is that a divergence is SURFACED — when a new plausible candidate appears for a telling that is already bound, `reaudit()` returns exactly one action, `mint_possible_overmerge`, naming the existing bind and the new candidate side by side. It can return nothing else; `FORBIDDEN_REAUDIT_ACTIONS` names move, split, keep, confirm, rebind and drop so a test can sweep every trigger and assert none is reachable. All nine enumerated triggers have a fixture and an unknown trigger is refused, so the enumeration cannot rot.\n\nONE DEFINITION, MANY HOSTS (ADR 0021). `IDENTITY_RULE_VERSION = \"event-identity:1\"` is introduced with exactly one home, and a test sweeps `system/*.py` and fails the build on a second assignment — that failure is the intended outcome and the fix is one import.\n\nEVERY NEGATIVE PROVEN TO FIRE. Each refusal is asserted in both directions — the state that trips it and the neighbouring state that must not — and the branch was additionally swept with eighteen deliberate one-line mutations of the two modules (pick the later of two `same` bindings; make era-bound claims groupable; let a containment override a value, become an anchor, or inherit the episode's provenance; let a merge leave a membership live; let the re-audit keep a bind; and so on). All eighteen turned the suite red. 84 new tests; the full suite stays green.\n\nContract: `docs/contracts/event-identity-i0-fold.md`, which also records the four places the design's §5 wording had to be pinned to be executable rather than papered over. Tracking lifehug#295 / lifehug-platform#781.",
